{"gap_id": "GAP-016", "category": "EG", "priority": "P1", "status": "OPEN", "description": "READ VREDENBURGH ET AL. 2009 (REF-01002) IN FULL. IT IS THE FIRST ANCHORING-TIER STATED MAXIMUM THIS PARAMETER HAS HAD, AND IT IS HELD AT SKIM GRADE. The TRID/TRB record and its verbatim abstract were retrieved and persisted this batch (HTTP 200, 50825 bytes) and give: Results suggest that for a transit distance of up to 20 feet, a ramp should not exceed a maximum cross slope of 5% or a maximum running slope of 7%. THE ARTICLE ITSELF WAS NOT RETRIEVED. Journal of Architectural and Planning Research 26(2):145-158, ISSN 0738-0895, NO DOI -- which is why six batches of DOI-keyed searching never met it and why it sat unread inside REF-00977 reference list. WHAT THE FULL READ MUST SETTLE, none of which the abstract answers: (1) the participant count and composition -- the record says users of manual AND motorised wheelchairs and states no n, so the population match is graded PARTIAL on an unknown denominator; (2) the slope set actually tested, since 7% may be a recommendation or merely the highest slope at which perceived effort did not degrade, and those are different claims; (3) whether the 20-foot transit distance is a tested bound or an assumption; (4) whether disabled people shaped the study beyond serving as subjects, which under D-0178 is the difference between T1 and Co-1. ON RESOLUTION: promote extraction 45 from skim/preliminary, resolve determination gate 1, and re-determine parameter 3 x MOB -- or record that the abstract overstated. UNTIL THEN THE FIGURE DOES NOT MOVE. Specification 8 stands at 5% and is SAFE rather than correct: 7% is LOOSER, the parameter direction is lower_is_better, so the standing value errs conservative while its tier_basis (T4-6-only-threshold, rests_on_proxy_inference=1) describes the wrong evidence. Batch 15 refused to claim Co-1 for REF-01001 on an ambiguous abstract and the full text vindicated the refusal; this is the same discipline one column along. TRID also lists the publisher as Elsevier Science, which is wrong for this journal -- do not propagate it; the publisher field was left unwritten for that reason.", "created_at": "2026-09-18 20:03", "created_by_session": "session_2026-09-18-research-batch-16-backward-mining-t2-anchors", "updated_at": "2026-09-18 20:03", "updated_by_session": "session_2026-09-18-research-batch-16-backward-mining-t2-anchors"}
---
{"gap_id": "GAP-018", "category": "RP", "priority": "P2", "status": "CLOSED-DECIDED", "description": "THE PROJECT RANKED ITS OWN MINING TARGETS AND GOT THEM EXACTLY BACKWARDS, TWICE, AND THE PREDICTOR THAT WOULD HAVE WORKED IS CHEAP. Batch 15 deferred three anchors with explicit rankings: REF-00984 (a systematic review of wheelchair SKILLS TESTS) HIGHEST-VALUE TARGET, REF-00985 (a biomechanics paper) LOWEST expected yield. Measured this batch against the same ramp/slope/gradient screen: REF-00984 yielded 0 of 38, REF-00985 yielded 23 of 46 and produced both of the batch anchoring-tier admissions. My own pre-registered priors were wrong in the same two places and in one further way -- I predicted REF-00984 would yield test-apparatus numbers needing a guard, and it yielded no slope references at all. THE REGULARITY IS DERIVABLE AND NEITHER SESSION DERIVED IT: a paper cites the literature of ITS OWN INDEPENDENT VARIABLE. REF-00985 measures propulsion effort, so it cites propulsion-on-slopes work; REF-00984 reviews measurement INSTRUMENTS, so it cites reliability, scoring and construct validity. Topical proximity of the ANCHOR to the slug predicted nothing; what the anchor MEASURES predicted everything. WHAT IS OWED: this is a hypothesis from two observations and must not become doctrine on that basis. Test it prospectively -- record a predicted yield for each deferred anchor BEFORE the pass, from what the anchor measures rather than what it is about, and check the prediction after. If it holds over a further three or four passes it belongs in the citation-miner skill relevance filter, which today screens DISCOVERIES for relevance and says nothing about ranking ANCHORS. A second, cheaper finding rides along and should be tested with it: 3 of the 3 best finds this batch (REF-01002 admitted, plus candidates 102 and 103) carry NO DOI in the deposit, so a DOI-keyed screen would have missed all three.", "created_at": "2026-09-18 20:03", "created_by_session": "session_2026-09-18-research-batch-16-backward-mining-t2-anchors", "updated_at": "2026-09-18 22:09", "updated_by_session": "session_2026-09-18-research-batch-17-vredenburgh-full-read-and-mining"}
